267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Recently, a middle school teacher sought to demonstrate the power of words. She placed two apples in brown paper bags on opposite corners of her classroom. Each morning for several days, she had her students gather around one bag and hurl insults at it. They would then surround the second bag and speak lovingly and compassionately to it, offering words of affirmation and affection. After several days, they opened the bags. The apple that had received insults was found to be rotten and mold-ridden. The apple that had received praise looked as fresh and crisp as day one. We often hear that our words have power, and we see this in Scripture, but how many believers truly believe it? And if we do not believe God's Word, can we actually call ourselves believers?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (So, how much power do our words actually have? Are we able to manifest a reality through positive confession or bring sickness upon ourselves through negative repetition? The biblical answer is both yes and no. The Bible teaches us that we will eat the fruit of our words. When we speak Gods word, we will reap his blessings. When we speak words of the flesh or of the enemy we will reap carnality, sickness and death. Whose words do you speak?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
