361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E ( Perhaps one of the most difficult realities concerning communication not only as a leader but as a christian is to truly practice treating others as we ought to be treated, not as we are being treated.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Grace of Saint Stephen)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In the early days of the Christian church, persecution was rampant. Among the first to face the ultimate test of faith was Saint Stephen, a man full of faith and the Holy Spirit. Chosen as one of the seven deacons to serve the early Christian community, Stephen was known for his powerful preaching and miraculous works, which drew the ire of the Jewish authorities.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Stephen's unwavering commitment to Christ and his bold proclamation of the Gospel led to his arrest and trial before the Sanhedrin, the Jewish ruling council. As false witnesses accused him, Stephen remained composed, filled with divine grace. He responded not with anger or retaliation, but with a profound and courageous speech that recounted Israel's history and pointed to Jesus as the Righteous One.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (His words, inspired by the Holy Spirit, cut to the heart of his accusers. Enraged, they dragged Stephen out of the city and began to stone him. In this brutal moment, Stephen's response was not one of bitterness or condemnation. Instead, he embodied the very essence of Christlike grace. As the stones rained down upon him, Stephen prayed, "Lord Jesus, receive my spirit." Then, echoing the words of Jesus on the cross, he cried out, "Lord, do not hold this sin against them" \(Acts 7:59-60, ESV\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (As we dive into this week's lesson on "Speaking with Grace," let us draw inspiration from Saint Stephen. His ability to extend grace and forgiveness, even in the direst of circumstances, exemplifies the call to speak to others not merely as they treat us but as Christ would have us treat them. Our words hold the power to heal, uplift, and reflect the love of Jesus, making "Speaking with Grace" an essential practice for every Christian leader.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
